# Deployment Workflow

## Branch policy

This repository does not use working branches or pull requests. Commit on `main` and push `main` directly. This overrides any general rule that keeps `main` read-only.

## Trigger a new Docker image build

When asked to "trigger a new image build" or "trigger a build", run all steps in one pass:

1. Check `pyproject.toml` for the current version
2. Bump the version (patch/minor/major as appropriate) in `pyproject.toml` and in the `docker-compose.yml` image tag
3. Commit the version bump on `main`
4. Push `main`
5. Tag with `v<version>` and push the tag: `git tag vX.Y.Z && git push origin vX.Y.Z`
6. This triggers `.github/workflows/docker-publish.yml`

## GA / Release

When the user says "GA" or asks to create a release:

1. Create a GitHub Release for the latest tag using `gh release create`
2. Generate the description by reviewing commits since the previous tag:
   ```bash
   gh release create vX.Y.Z --title "vX.Y.Z — Summary" --notes "$(cat <<'EOF'
   ...release notes from commits...
   EOF
   )"
   ```
3. Follow the same format as previous releases: `## What's New` / `## Bug Fixes` sections

## Non-code changes

For changes that touch only `.cursor/rules/`, `README.md`, `.env.example`, or similar docs — commit on `main` and push, but do NOT bump the version or tag (no Docker build needed).
